  "worked_solution": "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{\u2032}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve $f(y)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ -(y-3)({y}^{2}+6y+9) & =0 \\\\ -(y-3)(y+3)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y & =-3,3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solutions are $y=-3$ and $y=3.$\n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-3)$ | $(-3,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-(y-3)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| $(y+3)^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,3),$ and negative for $y\\in (3,\\infty ).$\n\nWe can now draw the phase line for this equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/3c118c900486d8561864c88e1b25a461c8f459f810bb7b753d30d9d0d4780913.png)\n\nFinally, we classify the equilibrium solutions:\n\n- $y=-3$  is a node because it is neither a source nor a sink.\n\n- $y=3$ is a sink because the nearby solutions converge toward this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$y=-3$ is a node, and $y=3$ is a sink\".",
